      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 4 of 7 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 (water meters, electromagnetic). The fragment contains two pages (page 8 and 9 of 16).\n\nKey content:\n\n1. Certificate number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 — this is a scheme... R49 certificate. The scheme designation \"A\" appears in the number (R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02). Actually the format suggests scheme A. But the fragment doesn't explicitly say scheme A — the certificate number contains \"-A-\". I could include scheme \"A\" derived from the number. Hmm, rule says don't fabricate. The number R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02 — \"A\" between 2013 and CZ1 strongly suggests scheme A. I'll include it since it's in the number. Actually, let me be careful — this is a fragment; other parts may contain the certificate header. I should extract what's present. The certificate number is present in both page headers, so I can include `certificate.number`. Scheme can be inferred from the number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" — the \"-A-\" segment. I'll include scheme: \"A\".\n\n2. Manufacturer: Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy. So manufacturers[]: name \"Hemina SPA\", address_lines [\"Via Piemonte 2\", \"Montagnana\", \"350 44 Italy\"]. Actually the string is semicolon separated: \"Hemina SPA; Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\".\n\n3. Model number: MS2500 / MV110. So certified_type.type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"] — the model. Note \"MS2500 / MV110\" seems to be the meter (MS2500) / converter (MV110)? Actually in the power supply section, \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\": MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV110 LV, MS2500 / MV110 LLV. These look like power supply variants: HV (high voltage, 100-240 V AC), LV (24-36 V AC/DC), LLV (10-48 V DC).\n\n4. Nominal diameter: 100, 125, 150 (three columns). DN values in mm presumably.\n\n5. Flow characteristics table: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 for each of 3 nominal diameters × 2 Q3/Q1 ratios? Let's parse:\n\nThe table has a \"Nominal diameter\" row with three values: 100, 125, 150 — each spanning 2 columns (colspan=2 each). Then rows Q1..Q4 have 6 values each:\n\n- Q1: 1.00, 0.63, 1.60, 1.00, 2.52, 1.60\n- Q2: 1.60, 1.00, 2.56, 1.60, 4.03, 1.56\n- Q3: 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630\n- Q4: 313, 313, 500, 500, 780, 780\n- Q3/Q1: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400\n\nSo columns pair as: DN100 with Q3/Q1=250 (Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=250, Q4=313), DN100 with Q3/Q1=400 (Q1=0.63, Q2=1.00, Q3=250, Q4=313), DN125 with Q3/Q1=250 (Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500), DN125 with Q3/Q1=400 (Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, Q3=400, Q4=500), DN150 with Q3/Q1=250 (Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, Q3=630, Q4=780), DN150 with Q3/Q1=400 (Q1=1.60, Q2=1.56, Q3=630, Q4=780).\n\nSo the variants are combos of nominal diameter × Q3/Q1 ratio (250 or 400). Also accuracy class row: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2 — so each DN variant has both class 1 and class 2? Hmm, the accuracy class row shows \"1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2\" per column. That seems odd — each variant could be class 1 and class 2. Actually it might mean the meters are approved as both class 1 and class 2? Or the column corresponds... Hmm. Let me reconsider: For each DN, there might be two variants: one class 1 (with Q3/Q1=250) and one class 2 (with Q3/Q1=400)? But Q3/Q1 row says 250, 400 for each pair. Accuracy class row says 1, 2. So possibly: DN100 Q3/Q1=250 is class 1, DN100 Q3/Q1=400 is class 2, DN125 Q3/Q1=250 class 1, DN125 Q3/Q1=400 class 2, DN150 Q3/Q1=250 class 1, DN150 Q3/Q1=400 class 2.\n\nThat makes sense: class 1 requires Q3/Q1 ≥ 250? No wait, R49 class 1 vs 2: Class 1 meters Q3/Q1 ≥ 250? Actually R49-1 classes: accuracy class 1 or 2, and Q3/Q1 ratio. Class 1 meters must have Q3/Q1 of at least 160? Hmm. Let me think: In ISO 4064 / OIML R49, Q3/Q1 can be 10, 16, 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630. Both classes can have various ratios. But the pattern here strongly suggests each variant is defined by (DN, class): DN100/250 class1, DN100/400 class2? Hmm, but why would class 2 need higher Q3/Q1? Class 1 is more accurate, so it would need higher ratio... Actually no — accuracy class is about MPE; Q3/Q1 is the flow range ratio. A class 1 meter typically needs larger dynamic range to meet accuracy over range... Not necessarily.\n\nAlternatively, the columns might represent: for each DN there are two approved configurations: class 1 with Q3/Q1=250 and class 2 with Q3/Q1=400. Hmm, that seems backwards (class 1 should be the better one). Wait, class 1 has tighter MPE (±1% upper zone vs ±2%). For class 1, you'd think Q3/Q1=400 (wider range) would be harder. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe it's the other way: class 1 variant has Q3/Q1 = 250 and class 2 variant has Q3/Q1 = 400? That doesn't fit \"class 2 is worse so can have wider range\". Actually wider range ratio is harder, so class 2 (looser) could achieve wider ratio. That fits: class 2 with Q3/Q1=400, class 1 with Q3/Q1=250. Hmm interesting but plausible.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider: Q1 for DN100: variant A (Q3/Q1=250): Q1=1.00; variant B (Q3/Q1=400): Q1=0.63. Q3=250 both. So variant B has lower Q1 = wider range = class 2? The MPE row says \"±3 % (Class 1), ±5 % (Class 2)\" for lower zone and \"±1 (class 1) ±2 (class 2) % for T ≤ 30°C, ±2 (class1) ±3 (class 2) % for T > 30°C\" for upper zone. These are generic MPE definitions from R49-1 for both classes, applying to the whole certificate. So the certificate covers both classes.\n\nThe safest interpretation: 6 variants = DN (100/125/150) × Q3/Q1 ratio (250/400), with accuracy class being 1 for ratio 250 columns and 2 for ratio 400 columns based on the column alignment (1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2). The row has 6 separate cells with 1,2,1,2,1,2 — each aligned with one of the six columns. So yes, per-column accuracy class.\n\nSo model matrix: I'll construct model family with models identified by e.g. \"DN100_Q3/Q1=250\" or \"DN100, Q3/Q1=250\". Model_id strings. Then model_level characteristics: nominal_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1,