50; For liner Ebonite - T50; T30 from DN200; For liner polypropylene - T50; T30\". This is messy — config_level with axis \"liner\": values with string values. E.g. {condition: \"PTFE\", value: \"T90, T50, T30\"}, {condition: \"Rilsan\", value: \"T50, T30 from DN50\"}, {condition: \"Ebonite\", value: \"T50, T30 from DN200\"}, {condition: \"polypropylene\", value: \"T50, T30\"}.\n\n- Water pressure class: MAP16 — value \"MAP16\", string.\n- Pressure loss class: Δp40 for all columns — value \"Δp40\" (string). Per-DN same. Type-level.\n- Reverse flow: \"designed to measure\" — string.\n- Environmental class: \"O (-25 to +55 °C)\" — could be {value: {min:-25,max:55}, unit \"°C\"} plus environmental_class \"O\". I'll record as string value or split. Maybe characteristic environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} and separate. Simpler: value \"O (-25 to +55 °C)\" as string. Hmm, rules say structured values. I could do two characteristics: environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} and environmental_temperature_range: {min:-25,max:55} °C. That's cleaner.\n- Electromagnetic environment: \"E1, E2\" — string value \"E1, E2\".\n- Mechanical class: \"B, O\" — string.\n- Maximum admissible temperature: config by liner: PTFE 90°C; Polypropylen, Rilsan, Ebonite 50°C. config_level: attribute \"maximum_admissible_temperature\", axis \"liner\", values: [{condition \"PTFE\", value 90, unit °C}, {condition \"polypropylene, Rilsan and Ebonite\", value 50, unit °C}].\n- Maximum admissible pressure: 1.6 MPa.\n- Orientation limitation: any.\n- Indicating range: 999999 m³ (all columns).\n- Resolution of indicating device: 0.001 m³.\n- Resolution of device for rapid testing: \"-\" → not present, omit (empty).\n- EUT testing requirements: category \"Electromagnetic water meter\", case \"Case D\".\n- Installation details: connection type (screw thread): Flanged; minimum straight length inlet pipe: 0 mm; outlet pipe: 0 mm; flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0; flow conditioner: No; mounting: \"-\"; orientation: any; other: \"-\"; length [mm]: 250, 250, 300 — per DN (three values for DN100, DN125, DN150). So model_level characteristic: length_mm per DN model... but our models are DN × ratio combos. Length is per DN: DN100 → 250, DN125 → 250, DN150 → 300. I'll add model_level entry with values per model for all 6 models (repeat per ratio variant) or... Hmm. The models are 6 variants; length applies to both variants of same DN. I'll list values for all 6 model_ids. Or maybe keep models at DN level? But flow values differ per ratio variant.\n\nWait — reconsider the model identification. Since accuracy class aligns per column too, maybe models are (DN, class). But Q3/Q1 differs. Let me define model_id as \"DN100_Q3/Q1=250_class1\" — too verbose. Perhaps \"DN100, Q3/Q1 250, class 1\". Hmm.\n\nActually let me reconsider alignment: columns for Q1: 1.00 | 0.63 | 1.60 | 1.00 | 2.52 | 1.60. DN header spans columns 1-2 (100), 3-4 (125), 5-6 (150). Q3/Q1 row: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400 — separate cells per column. Accuracy class row: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2 — separate cells. So each of the 6 columns = one variant: (DN100, ratio 250, class 1), (DN100, ratio 400, class 2), (DN125, ratio 250, class 1), (DN125, ratio 400, class 2), (DN150, ratio 250, class 1), (DN150, ratio 400, class 2).\n\nHmm wait, but is it class 1 with ratio 250? That pairing seems odd from a metrology standpoint but the data is the data. Alternatively the accuracy class row might mean \"class 1 and 2\" for all columns (i.e., meters can be class 1 or class 2). But it's formatted as 6 separate cells aligned to columns, whereas rows that apply to all use colspan=6. So per-column it is.\n\nI'll create 6 models:\n- model_id: \"DN100, Q3/Q1=250, class 1\"? That's long. Maybe \"DN100-250-C1\". Hmm. Better to keep readable: \"DN100 Q3/Q1=250 class 1\".\n\nActually, since the DN100/250/class1 vs DN100/400/class2 — hmm, let me double check Q1 for DN100 ratio 400: 0.63 → 250/0.63 = 396.8 ≈ 400 ✓. Q2 = 1.00 → Q2/Q1 = 1.587 ≈ 1.6 ✓. For ratio 250: Q1=1.00, 250/1 = 250 ✓.\n\nDN125 ratio 250: Q1=1.60 → 400/1.6 = 250 ✓. Ratio 400: Q1=1.00 → 400/1.0 = 400 ✓. Q2=1.60 → 1.6 ✓.\n\nDN150 ratio 250: Q1=2.52 → 630/2.52 = 250 ✓. Ratio 400: Q1=1.60 → 630/1.6 ≈ 394 ≈ 400 ✓ (approximately; Q2=1.56 gives Q2/Q1 ≈ 0.975?? That's odd — Q2/Q1 should be 1.6. 1.56/1.60 = 0.975. Hmm, that's likely an OCR/transcription error in the certificate, or Q2 = 2.56. Indeed DN125 ratio250 Q2 = 2.56; DN150 ratio 400 Q2 should be 2.56 for Q1=1.6. The value 1.56 seems like an error but I must not fabricate — record 1.56 as given.)\n\nOK so model matrix:\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter: unit \"mm\"? The header says \"Nominal diameter: 100, 125, 150\" — DN is dimensionless label but effectively mm. I'll use unit_symbol \"mm\" (DN100 = 100 mm nominal diameter). Actually safer: unit null and model ids carry DN. Hmm. The characteristic \"nominal_diameter\" values 100, 125, 150 with unit \"mm\". I'll go with \"mm\".\n- q1: unit \"m³/h\", values per model: 1.00, 0.63, 1.60, 1.00, 2.52, 1.60\n- q2: 1.60, 1.00, 2.56, 1.60, 4.03, 1.56\n- q3: 250, 250, 400, 400, 630, 630\n- q4: 313, 313, 500, 500, 780, 780\n- q3_over_q1: 250, 400, 250, 400, 250, 400 (dimensionless)\n- accuracy_class: 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2 (dimensionless)\n- length: unit \"mm\": per model: 250 (DN100 both variants), 250 (DN125 both), 300 (DN150 both)\n\nmatrix_tables: I could also emit a matrix table \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter and Q3/Q1 ratio\" with columns: nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, q3_over_q1, q1, q2, q3, q4, units m³/h for flows. And a second one for the power supply modules.\n\nPower supply: three modules: MS2500/MV110 HV, MS2500/MV110 LV, MS2500/MV110 LLV.\n- HV: Type: 100 – 240 V AC; Frequency: 45 – 66 Hz; Internal backup source: 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\n- LV: 24 – 36V AC/DC; 45 – 66 Hz; backup: 3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh; Battery\n- LLV: 10 – 48 V DC; frequency: -; backup: -\n\nThese could be config_level (axis: power supply module / voltage variant) or components. They're variants of the same instrument configuration. I'll use config_level entries:\n- power_supply_voltage: axis \"power_supply_module\": [{condition: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", value: {min:100, max:240}, unit: \"V\", plus type AC}, {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", value: {min:24, max:36}, unit \"V\", type \"AC/DC\"}, {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", value: {min:10, max:48}, unit \"V\", type \"DC\"}]\n- power_supply_frequency: HV 45-66 Hz, LV 45-66 Hz, LLV none.\n- power_supply_type: AC / AC/DC / DC.\n- internal_backup_source: \"3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh battery\" for HV and LV, none for LLV.\n\nNote decimal: \"3,7V\" → 3.7.\n\n- minimum_battery_life_time: \"-\" → omit.\n- software_version: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\" — string value.\n- CRC checksum: \"9BA34F3B\" — string.\n- cut_off: 0,2% → value 0.2, unit \"%\" — attribute \"cut_off\".\n- detection_empty_pipe: \"on\" — string.\n\nType-level:\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- mpe config entries\n- temperature_class config by liner\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\" — string. Could do value \"Δp40\". The Δ character — fine to keep \"Δp40\".\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\", environmental_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: 999999 m³\n- resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.001 m³\n- eut_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_case: \"Case D\" → value \"D\"? I'll do eut_test_case: \"Case D\" or value \"D\". Keep \"D\".\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- orientation: \"any\"\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — the R49 category. The certificate is for water meters; the fragment says \"Electromagnetic water meter\" as measuring principle and EUT category. Category field examples list includes \"Measuring system\", \"Gas meter\"... For R49 it's \"Water meter\". I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\"]\n- module_designation: null — not applicable for water meters; omit or null. I'll omit... The schema has module_designation field with options \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. This fragment doesn't mention modules. I'll omit it since fragment-only. Hmm, but \"Designation of the module for all nominal diameter\" — that's power supply module designations. Actually the MS2500 could be the meter and MV110 the converter (electromagnetic flowmeters often consist of sensor + converter). \"MS2500 / MV110\" might mean meter MS2500 with converter MV110. But I shouldn't fabricate. Keep type_designations [\"MS2500 / MV110\"]. Maybe add components? Not clearly defined. I'll skip components.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\" (from number segment). Hmm — I'll include it since the number format \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" indicates scheme A (the pattern R49/2013-A-...). I think it's justified.\n- recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013. The number contains R49/2013 which is the edition. Recommendation canonical: \"R49\". Edition: 2013. I'll include. Accuracy classes: [1, 2]. From the table. Include.\n- page_total: 16 (\"Page 8 of 16 pages\") — the fragment shows \"of 16 pages\". Include page_total: 16. That's present in the fragment.\n\nrecommendation.scheme: \"A\"? The certificate scheme A. The scheme field is under certificate and also under recommendation per schema. I'll put scheme \"A\" under certificate. Maybe also under recommendation. The example schema shows recommendation has scheme. I'll include both.\n\nrevision_history: \"Verze 20-001\" is a form version, not a certificate revision. Skip.\n\nfootnotes: none present.\n\nIssuing authority: Czech Metrology Institute (Český metrologický institut) appears in stamp — but it's a stamp note; risky to fabricate. The certificate number contains CZ1. The stamp says \"Český metrologický institut\". Should I include issuing_authority? The stamp is visible: '[Blue round stamp, partly visible: \"Český metrologický institut\" around the Czech lion coat of arms]'. This is evidence of issuer. I could include name: \"Český metrologický institut\" (Czech Metrology Institute). It's arguably present. But rule 8 says don't fabricate; the stamp text is present in the markdown. I'll include issuing_authority.name as \"Český metrologický institut\" — it's literally in the fragment. Hmm, but it's an image marker-ish description. I think including it is defensible; the extractor should capture the issuer. I'll include name only.\n\nApplicants: only \"Manufacturer:\" listed. No applicant row in this fragment. The manufacturer: Hemina SPA. Include manufacturers[] only.\n\nNow model_ids: I need consistent IDs across model_level entries. Let me define:\n- \"DN100_Q3Q1_250_class1\"? Readability: \"DN100 Q3/Q1=250 class 1\". JSON strings can contain / and =. I'll use \"DN100 Q3/Q1=250 (class 1)\". Hmm simpler: \"DN100 Q3/Q1 250 class 1\". Let me pick format: \"DN100_Q3/Q1=250_class1\". Slightly ugly. Alternative: use combo \"DN100-250\" and note class per accuracy_class attribute. Models: \"DN100 Q3/Q1=250\", \"DN100 Q3/Q1=400\", etc. And accuracy_class as model_level attribute. That keeps ids short. Good.\n\nModels:\n1. \"DN100 Q3/Q1=250\"\n2. \"DN100 Q3/Q1=400\"\n3. \"DN125 Q3/Q1=250\"\n4. \"DN125 Q3/Q1=400\"\n5. \"DN150 Q3/Q1=250\"\n6. \"DN150 Q3/Q1=400\"\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- nominal_diameter: unit mm: 100,100,125,125,150,150\n- q1: m³/h: 1.00, 0.63, 1.60, 1.00, 2.52, 1.60\n- q2: 1.60, 1.00, 2.56, 1.60, 4.03, 1.56\n- q3: 250,250,400,400,630,630\n- q4: 313,313,500,500,780,780\n- q3_over_q1: 250,400,250,400,250,400\n- accuracy_class: 1,2,1,2,1,2\n-